    "avoid": {
      "type": "uc-csc-content",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "What to Avoid for a Stress-Free Delivery",
        "body": "<p><strong>Rough roads.</strong> Roads made of dirt, gravel, or with damaged pavement may not be suitable for shipping container delivery due to road weight limits and the risk of getting stuck, causing damage to the road and equipment, or being environmentally disruptive.</p><p><strong>Steep road inclines and declines.</strong> Steep inclines can make it impossible to haul a load uphill, while a steep decline can pose safety hazards to the delivery crew and the surrounding environment. Contact us to receive an evaluation of your delivery route and determine if your local roadway grades qualify for a successful delivery.</p><p><strong>Tight turns.</strong> Shipping containers require sufficient turning clearance to prevent getting stuck, causing damage, and for safety purposes. Delivery of a 20ft container requires a minimum of 60ft straight line clearance, while a 40ft container requires a minimum of 125ft straight line clearance to perform a safe and effective turn.</p><p><strong>Height restrictions.</strong> Any obstacle that does not offer at least 14ft of vertical clearance is considered a height restriction — including power lines, trees, gateways, arches, building eaves, and low bridges.</p><p><strong>Width restrictions.</strong> Shipping containers require a minimum of 14ft of horizontal clearance to pass through without getting stuck or causing delays. Ideally, roads leading to your location should be a standard 2-lane marked road at minimum.</p><p><strong>Weather-affected roadways.</strong> Icy, flooded, muddy, debris-covered, or unplowed snowy roads can make delivery impractical and dangerous. We're always happy to work with you to plan a delivery that accommodates your schedule and ensures a safe process.</p>",
        "primary_image_url": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1029/2284/6530/files/uc-delivery-power-lines.png?v=1789074149",
        "image_alt": "Overhead power lines height restriction"
      }
    },
    "methods": {
      "type": "uc-csc-content",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "Tilt Bed & Flatbed Delivery",
        "body": "<p><strong>Tilt bed delivery.</strong> Tilt bed trailers perform secure delivery with direct placement on the ground at the drop-off location. They're ideal for small businesses, private properties, and locations without specialized forklifts or cranes. Tilt beds can haul 20ft and 40ft containers, and because the trailer is pulled out from underneath the container, this method requires a minimum of 125ft straight line clearance. You'll also need to decide which way the doors will face before delivery.</p><p><strong>Flatbed delivery.</strong> Unlike tilt beds, flatbed trailers don't have their own off-loading feature — any container delivered on a flatbed must be off-loaded manually using a crane, forklift, or other heavy lifting equipment. Flatbed delivery is intended for business locations or properties with dedicated heavy lifting equipment on site.</p>",
        "primary_image_url": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1029/2284/6530/files/uc-delivery-stacked-containers.png?v=1789074149",
        "image_alt": "Stacked shipping containers"
      }
    },
